-- title:
--   Reducedness of the q^m-torsion of the Néron identity component
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ and $q$ be primes, let $m$ be a natural number, and let $N$ be a term of the structure `JZeroNeronIdentityComponent p`. Thus $N$ consists of a scheme $G$ of universe level $0$ together with a morphism $g : G \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Z}$, a relative group law $L$ on $g$ (a functorial group structure, natural in the base, on the sets $\{\varphi : T \to G \mid \varphi \text{ over } t\}$ of sections over arbitrary $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Z}$), an equivalence `pts` between $\mathrm{JZero}\,p = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the modular function field of level $p$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and the sections of $g$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, which is additive, Galois-equivariant and compatible with the Hecke action through endomorphisms of $g$ respecting the group law, together with the hypotheses that $L$ is commutative and that $g$ is smooth, separated, locally of finite type, quasi-compact and surjective with preconnected fibres, that the multiplication morphisms $[n] : G \to G$ (the $n$-fold $L$-sum of the identity point) are flat and surjective for $n > 0$, and that the subgroup generated by the integral sections has finite index in the Galois-fixed part of $\mathrm{JZero}\,p$. The conclusion is that the scheme $G[q^m]$, defined as the fibre product of $[q^m] : G \to G$ with the unit section $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Z} \to G$ of $L$, is reduced.
--
--   This is the assertion that the $q^m$-torsion subgroup scheme of the Néron identity component of $J_0(p)$ over $\mathbb{Z}$ has no nilpotents, the integral analogue of Cartier's theorem that a group scheme of finite type over a field of characteristic zero is reduced, transported to $\mathbb{Z}$ through flatness of $[q^m]$. It is used in the constructions of Hecke-equivariant idempotents, retracts and sections on torsion kernels of this group scheme.
